    "basis_mode": "model_knowledge",
    "model_knowledge_rationale": "Retrieved evidence confirms angular dependence and blue shift but does not provide specific thickness ratios for optimizing unpolarized TiO2/SiO2 stacks at 45 degrees. This route relies on general thin-film physics: at oblique incidence, the admittance of s and p polarizations diverges. A standard quarter-wave stack (optical thickness = lambda/4) designed for normal incidence will have mismatched phase conditions for s and p waves at 45 degrees, leading to split stopbands. By empirically adjusting the physical thickness of the high-index layer (dH) while keeping dL constant (or vice versa), one attempts to find a compromise thickness that maximizes the constructive interference overlap for both polarizations in the target visible band. This is a heuristic optimization strategy common in filter design when exact analytic solutions for unpolarized oblique incidence are complex.",
